## Use Case for eu-ehds:ServiceSupplyStatus
Description: This example illustrates the formal representation of the overarching regulatory standing of a cross-border Diagnostic System 
within the Union’s central registry. In this example, we model the continuous monitoring of a technology's presence in the market by a 
National Market Surveillance Authority. The core entity is the concept eu-ehds:ServiceSupplyStatus, which serves as the anchor for the technology's
presence in the EU supply chain. Rather than marking a single event, this concept functions as a state-machine anchor that tracks whether an EHR system
is legally authorized to be supplied, put into service, or if it must be recalled. The process is governed by a tech:hasProvisionStatus property 
that links the system’s supply-chain readiness to the acquisition of infrastructure level data like tech:Software for integrity verification. 
By using this specific status term, the model reflects the technical precision required for high-level governance, ensuring the technology is 
legally authorised for sector-health:DiagnosticSupport across jurisdictions.

## Use Case for eu-ehds:PutIntoService
Description: This example demonstrates the formal representation of the operational debut of an AI-driven Diagnostic Imaging Analysis 
system into a regulated clinical environment under the EHDS framework. This example models where a technology transitions from a development 
or testing state to being actively available for clinical decision-making. The core entity is the concept eu-ehds:PutIntoService, 
which serves as the legal and technical status anchor for the system’s entry into the healthcare market. The process is initiated as an 
implementation by a dpv:DataController, targeting the diagnostic processing of imaging data as a specialized 
task within sector-health:DiagnosisManagement. By using this specific status term, the model reflects the technical precision required to 
prove that the system has met all necessary interoperability and safety benchmarks required by EHDS Annex II before its first use.

## Use Case for eu-ehds:RecalledFromService
Description: This example illustrates the formal representation of a mandatory safety intervention involving a Remote Patient 
Vitals Monitoring component that has been flagged for a critical defect. Following the market surveillance requirements of the 
EHDS, the use case models the emergency switch triggered when a technology is no longer deemed safe for its intended purpose. 
The core entity is the concept eu-ehds:RecalledFromService, which serves as the authoritative status anchor that programmatically halts 
the system's operational life across all connected healthcare providers. The process involves a coordinated dpv:OrganisationalMeasure to 
execute hardware deactivation and return protocols, specifically targeting the immediate cessation of sector-health:PatientMonitoring. 
By using this specific status term (a sub-concept of the EHDS supply chain hierarchy), the model reflects the technical precision needed 
to isolate and neutralise faulty data streams in real-time to protect patient safety.

## Use Case for eu-ehds:WithdrawnFromService
Description: This example demonstrates the formal representation of the retirement of a Legacy Patient Portal that fails to 
satisfy the modern cybersecurity or interoperability standards mandated by the EHDS. Following the principle of Data Protection by Design,
the example models the removal of a technology from the supply chain to prevent the processing of high-risk datasets. The core entity 
is the concept eu-ehds:WithdrawnFromService, which serves as the terminal status anchor for the system’s lifecycle to ensure no further acquisition 
occurs. The process is characterized by a replacement logic where the legacy system is superseded by a secure portal, specifically to protect 
sensitive categories such as pd:MedicalHealth, pd:Prescription, and pd:MentalHealth. By using this specific status term, the model reflects the 
technical precision required to manage infrastructure modernisation while maintaining the continuity of sector-health:PatientInformationManagement.
